  {
    "id": "pus-9fc6e508-7019-4faa-8d94-08502a5b42b8",
    "title": "Kathmandu to Jiri : A Photo Journey (1968-2005)",
    "author": "Anupama Mahat, Bharat Pokharel",
    "language": "en",
    "country": "np",
    "source": "pustakalaya-other-educational",
    "sourceUrl": "https://pustakalaya.org/documents/detail/9fc6e508-7019-4faa-8d94-08502a5b42b8/",
    "coverUrl": "https://pustakalaya.org/media/uploads/op/thumbnail/MnDRB2VxVlg3enoV5GTQ4GgBI3GbY5.gif",
    "category": "Other Educational Materials",
    "keywords": [
      "Nepal Swiss Community Forestry Project",
      "Anupama Mahat",
      "Bharat Pokharel",
      "Jiri"
    ],
    "scrapedAt": "2026-05-24T11:34:44.382540Z",
    "readUrl": "https://pustakalaya.org/media/uploads/op/pdf/SDC2009_KathmanduToJiriAPhotoJourney.pdf/SDC2009_KathmanduToJiriAPhotoJourney.pdf",
    "fileSize": "11.16 MB",
    "publisher": "Nepal Swiss Community Forestry Project, SDC, Intercooperation, Nepal",
    "pageCount": "51",
    "description": "This book consists of 19 pairs of photos that clearly show changes in landscape over time. These photos were taken in various years and seasons, as mentioned in the captions\r\n          given for the respective photo. Each of these photos is purposefully chosen to depict changes along the way from Kathmandu to Jiri. These places can be located whilst driving along the\r\n          Lamosangu (Sindhupalchowk) - Jiri (Dolakha) highway. These are remarkable landscapes that show how influential community forestry institutions - mainly Community Forest Users’ Groups\r\n          (CFUGs) – have been in restoring the denuded hills, protecting forests and promoting agro-forestry. This collection goes on to show how forests have helped regenerate water bodies, thus\r\n          increasing soil fertility and promoting agriculture productivity through the forest-agriculture interface. Some of these collections are proof of how, despite urbanization, community\r\n          forestry institutions have played a role in increasing forest coverage. The collection also emphasises some of the aesthetic quality of green hills and other environmental services that\r\n          these forests provide. The biological control of landslides and soil erosion through Community Forestry is one of the remarkable achievements of community efforts, as highlighted in several\r\n          pictures."
  },
